Subject: elementary genetics

Dale:
> (Evolutionary quiz: Why do [some] organisms that reproduce asexually split
> a mature organism into parts, but organisms that reproduce sexually
> always rebuild a new organism from a single cell?)

Well, I can picture two flatworms peeling off haploid versions of all of
their cells, and letting these mate with their counterparts; I can't picture
it for a solid organism.

The result of such mating would be a chimera, since there's no reason for
meiosis to go the same way in each cell of the parent.  The survival of a
gene in a chimera is less tightly bound to its fitness than in animals of
the kind we know.  By a genetic sort of socialism, the chimerae would
rapidly lose out to our kind of sex.

