----

  If you decode the above by interpreting two spaces as a 1-bit and
one space as 0-bit, you will find "Raymond" hidden in there along with
some padding garbage. If your file is N bytes along, you will be able to 
hide ~1.7% N bytes. Good enough to hide a PGP keys, not very good for large
amounts of secret data.


Another crazy idea:

  Take GIF files and transform them into Luminance/Chrominance data.
Store your secret data as every other pixel in the chrominance channel.
You can get away with decimating the chrominance channel in half
without affecting the image seriously. This allows a 50% factor
in hiding data and doesn't increase the size of the image by much.

Crazy Idea #2 (voice hiding):

  Record you and a friend talking about boring stuff one night, or the
radio. Now play this data over and over again but hide your
Voice PGP conference in the LSBs of the data. That way, law enforcement
can CLIP your privacy ;-), but they won't hear the real conversation.

